 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Exponentially Ascending Gradient Threshold](9684-exponentially-ascending-gradient-threshold.md)
-A densification control mechanism that dynamically adjusts the sensitivity of densification decisions based on normalized training progress, solving the trade-off between early-stage convergence speed and late-stage overfitting.
+A densification control mechanism for 3D Gaussian Splatting that uses an exponentially ascending gradient threshold to regulate the splitting and cloning of Gaussians during training. It solves the trade-off between early-stage convergence speed and late-stage overfitting by allowing aggressive densification when few Gaussians exist and restricting it as the scene becomes dense. It sets itself apart from static threshold methods by dynamically adjusting the sensitivity of densification decisions based on the normalized training progress.
 
 ### [SplatFormer Training Objective & Refinement](11745-splatformer-training-objective-refinement.md)
-A learning-based, feed-forward 3D neural module that refines initial 3DGS representations to enable robust novel view synthesis from out-of-distribution (OOD) camera angles by predicting attribute residuals.
+SplatFormer is a learning-based, feed-forward 3D neural module that refines initial 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representations to enable robust novel view synthesis from out-of-distribution (OOD) camera angles [1, unofficial]. It addresses the bias of standard 3DGS optimization, which overfits to input views and fails to generalize to unseen high-elevation perspectives, by applying a point transformer network that predicts attribute residuals [1, unofficial]. Unlike 2D image restoration methods that suffer from multi-view inconsistencies, SplatFormer operates directly on 3D primitives, ensuring geometric consistency while recovering details lost in OOD views [1, unofficial].
 
 ### [MCMC-based 3DGS / SGLD for 3DGS](11917-mcmc-based-3dgs-sgld-for-3dgs.md)
-Reformulates 3DGS updates into Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics (SGLD) updates by introducing a noise term, removing the need for manual densification heuristics like cloning and splitting.
+This method reformulates 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by treating the set of 3D Gaussians as random samples drawn from an underlying probability distribution describing the scene, specifically as Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) samples [1]. It converts conventional 3DGS updates into Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics (SGLD) updates by introducing a noise term to promote sample exploration, thereby removing the need for manual densification heuristics like cloning, splitting, and opacity resetting [1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on complex gradient thresholds or hard constraints, this approach relies on probabilistic state transitions and regularization to manage Gaussian population [1].
 
 ### [Deblurring 3D Gaussian Splatting](12318-deblurring-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
-A real-time deblurring framework that reconstructs sharp 3D scenes from blurry input images by manipulating covariance matrices and means using a small MLP during training.
+Deblurring 3D Gaussian Splatting is a real-time deblurring framework that reconstructs sharp 3D scenes from blurry input images by manipulating the covariance matrices and means of 3D Gaussians using a small Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) during training [1]. Unlike volumetric rendering-based methods like Deblur-NeRF which require seconds per image, this method uses differentiable rasterization to achieve real-time inference speeds (>800 FPS) while handling both defocus and camera motion blur [1, 3]. It addresses the sparse point clouds caused by blurry inputs through K-nearest-neighbor interpolation and depth-based pruning [1].
 
 ### [Supplementary Point Addition for Sparse Point Clouds](12323-supplementary-point-addition-for-sparse-point-clouds.md)
-A densification technique that supplements the initial point cloud with synthetic points to compensate for sparsity in defocus-blurry environments where SfM extraction is poor.
+A densification technique designed to compensate for the sparsity of 3D Gaussians at the far end of scenes in defocus-blurry environments, where Structure-from-Motion (SfM) extraction is typically poor [1]. It supplements the initial point cloud with synthetic points to ensure adequate coverage in regions where SfM fails to extract features, thereby improving reconstruction fidelity in large-depth-of-field scenarios [1].
 
 ### [Augmented Radiance Field](13218-augmented-radiance-field.md)
-A post-enhancement framework that improves 3DGS scenes by adding Gaussian kernels with view-dependent opacity to explicitly model specular components, decoupling diffuse and specular radiance.
+Augmented Radiance Field is a post-enhancement framework that improves 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes by adding Gaussian kernels with view-dependent opacity to explicitly model specular components [1, unofficial]. It solves the problem that standard spherical harmonics (SH) cannot decouple diffuse and specular radiance, leading to poor reconstruction of sharp highlights and material artifacts [1, unofficial]. Unlike standard 3DGS which assigns SH coefficients to every primitive, this method uses distinct primitives for diffuse and specular components, avoiding the memory and stability issues of high-order SH [1, unofficial].
 
 ### [Frequency Regularization for Densification](13484-frequency-regularization-for-densification.md)
-Addresses over-reconstruction by minimizing the discrepancy between the frequency spectrum of the rendered image and the ground truth in Fourier space, guiding coarse-to-fine densification.
+Frequency Regularization for Densification (FreGS) addresses the over-reconstruction issue in 3D Gaussian Splatting, where high-variance image regions are covered by a few large Gaussians, leading to blur and artifacts [1]. Unlike standard 3D-GS which relies solely on pixel-wise L1 loss in the spatial domain, FreGS minimizes the discrepancy between the frequency spectrum of the rendered image and the ground truth in the Fourier space [1]. This approach significantly increases pixel gradients within over-reconstruction regions, enabling a coarse-to-fine densification process guided by progressive frequency annealing [1].
 
 ### [Error-Guided Redistribution](14037-error-guided-redistribution.md)
-Refines Gaussian allocation based on reconstruction quality metrics (positional gradients or SSIM), actively moving primitives to improve fidelity rather than just removing them.
+Error-Guided Redistribution is a family of training optimization methods for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that refine Gaussian allocation based on reconstruction quality metrics. It addresses artifacts caused by suboptimal primitive placement or size by either identifying defective Gaussians for targeted densification or reallocating a fixed budget of primitives toward high-error regions. It differs from standard densification by using explicit error signals (positional gradients or SSIM) rather than averaged gradient magnitudes, and from simple pruning by actively moving primitives to improve fidelity rather than just removing them.
 
 ### [CoherentGS](14072-coherentgs.md)
-A regularized optimization approach for sparse novel view synthesis that introduces coherency by constraining Gaussian movement in 2D image space, initialized with monocular depth estimates.
+CoherentGS is a regularized optimization approach for sparse novel view synthesis that introduces coherency to an explicit 3D Gaussian representation by constraining Gaussian movement in 2D image space, initialized with monocular depth estimates [1]. It addresses the instability and "needle-like" artifacts of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) when trained on extremely sparse inputs (e.g., 3–4 images) by enforcing single-view and multi-view constraints, preventing overfitting and allowing for the identification of occluded regions suitable for inpainting [1]. Unlike NeRF-based sparse methods that rely on implicit MLP coherency and produce blurry occluded regions, CoherentGS constrains explicit Gaussians in 2D image space, enabling sharper details and structured hallucination via diffusion models [1].
 
 ### [Pruning and Sparsity Control](14431-pruning-and-sparsity-control.md)
-Reduces the number of active 3D Gaussians to lower memory consumption by actively eliminating redundant primitives throughout the optimization process using explicit geometric or volume-based criteria.
+Pruning and Sparsity Control methods reduce the number of active 3D Gaussians in a radiance field to lower memory consumption and storage requirements while maintaining rendering quality [1, 12710-gaussian-volume-mask.md, unofficial]. Unlike standard 3DGS which relies on opacity thresholds and stops densifying mid-training, these methods actively eliminate redundant primitives throughout the optimization process [12710-gaussian-volume-mask.md]. They are distinguished from sibling methods like [Exponentially Ascending Gradient Threshold](9684-exponentially-ascending-gradient-threshold.md) by using explicit geometric or volume-based criteria rather than gradient magnitude alone [14019-visibility-based-pruning-for-3d-gaussian-splatting.md].
+
+### [Rendering-Free Pruning via Hessian Importance](14447-rendering-free-pruning-via-hessian-importance.md)
+Rendering-Free Pruning via Hessian Importance (REFINE) is a post-processing framework for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that identifies and removes unimportant Gaussian primitives without performing forward rendering passes [1]. It quantifies primitive importance using an analytically approximated Hessian field, which estimates the image-space quality degradation caused by removing a primitive based on parameter-space gradients [1]. Unlike rendering-based methods such as PUP [14431-pruning-and-sparsity-control.md] or LightGaussian, REFINE bypasses rasterization entirely, relying on closed-form algebraic operations to achieve a 3,000× reduction in computational complexity and a ~20× speedup in device latency [1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Memory:** The fine-tuning process significantly reduces the number of Gaussians compared to the initial feed-forward prediction, thereby reducing memory consumption [S1].
-*   **Time:** Convergence is accelerated compared to test-time optimization from scratch due to the use of pretrained priors [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** The fine-tuning phase involves iterative optimization, with complexity dependent on the number of Gaussians and training iterations. The feed-forward initialization is typically faster than iterative SfM [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the number of active Gaussians. Fine-tuning with pruning reduces memory consumption by eliminating redundant primitives [S1].
+- **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods, leveraging pretrained weights [S1].
+- **Scaling:** The method scales with the number of training images and the complexity of the scene. Pretrained priors accelerate convergence [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Faster convergence, reduced memory consumption through pruning, high fidelity [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the feed-forward initialization. Pruning thresholds require tuning [S1].
+- **When to use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction is needed with limited memory, or when pretrained priors can accelerate training [S1].
+- **When not to use:** When the feed-forward initialization is poor, or when real-time inference is the primary constraint and memory is not [S1].
 
